\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction degrades as schema complexity grows, yet prior work conflates two factors: type count and type confusability. We present what is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors across NER, RE, and event detection on four datasets, using the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and its batch-level aggregation (BCS) as operational tools. Three findings emerge.
(1)~Count degradation spans NER, RE, and ED (up to $-$49\,pp); confusability-driven type substitution is documented primarily in RE, with bounded interaction on FewRel.
(2)~In RE, BCS explains ${\sim}$22\% of cross-batch accuracy variance on FewRel ($R^2 = 0.218$, ecological level), replicating across three open-weight model families (8B--70B), two formats, and five prompts; NER batch-level signal is not detected at current power, revealing a task asymmetry.
(3)~Schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification (NER, ED) and routing failure (RE), though oracle routing confirms the two dimensions are independently addressable.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Schema complexity affects training-free LLM information extraction through two independent dimensions: type count drives recall decline, while inter-type confusability drives type substitution errors. We present what is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors in zero-shot LLM IE across NER, RE, and event detection on four datasets. Using embedding-based confusability metrics (SCS/BCS) with controlled batch design, we find:
(1)~count degradation spans all three tasks (up to $-$49\,pp); confusability-driven substitution is documented primarily in RE, with bounded interaction;
(2)~BCS explains ${\sim}$22\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance on FewRel (ecological level), replicating across three open-weight model families (8B--70B); NER batch-level signal is not detected at current power;
(3)~schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification and routing failure, though oracle routing confirms the two dimensions are independently addressable.
\end{abstract}

